# `!=`, `&&` and `!` together: the fixes applied together still parse.
mode: syntax
---- query
t | where a!=1&&!b
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..19
  PIPELINE@0..18
    NAME_REF@0..1
      IDENT@0..1 "t"
    WHITESPACE@1..2 " "
    PIPE@2..3 "|"
    WHITESPACE@3..4 " "
    WHERE_OP@4..18
      IDENT@4..9 "where"
      WHITESPACE@9..10 " "
      BIN_EXPR@10..18
        BIN_EXPR@10..14
          NAME_REF@10..11
            IDENT@10..11 "a"
          NEQ@11..13 "!="
          LITERAL@13..14
            LONG@13..14 "1"
        AMP2@14..16 "&&"
        PREFIX_EXPR@16..18
          BANG@16..17 "!"
          NAME_REF@17..18
            IDENT@17..18 "b"
  WHITESPACE@18..19 "\n"

error[DS0111]: `&&` is not an operator
  --> query:1:15
  |
1 | t | where a!=1&&!b
  |               ^^ this is C's "and"
  |
  = help: use `and` to require both conditions

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:17
  |
1 | t | where a!=1&&!b
  |                 ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(b)`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    t | where a!=1 and not(b)
which parses without errors
